                         MEDITATIONS ON SELFLESSNESS

Preface

In this present world where ‘self’ seems to be king; people
seek what is best for them even if it is at the detriment of
others. However, God’s love remains selfless at heart,
putting the interests and commands of God as utmost
priority and putting the interest of others ahead of one if
need be.

The whole essence of God’s commandments is Love. A
selfless life is an expression of an authentic Christian faith
and a vital measure of Christian maturity. Living selflessly
is a vital way to live out your faith as a believer.

These meditations, when applied effectively, will work a
selfless heart into you. Do well to find a suitable
environment and engage these meditations effectively.
Prayerfully confess these scriptures routinely and
continually.

It takes time for the things of the spirit to crystalise, so
these meditations will become tangible in your life after a
lengthy time of use.
 Meditations On

 Selflessness

People say, 'An eye for eye, and a tooth for a tooth.',
but I don’t not repay the evil person with evil. If
someone strikes me on the right cheek, I turn to him
the left also. If someone wants to sue me and take my
tunic, I quickly let him have my cloak as well. If
someone forces me to go one mile, I go two miles. I
give to the one who asks me and do not turn away
from the one who wants to borrow from me.
(Matt. 5:38-43 KJV paraphrased).

People say, 'Love your neighbour and hate your
enemy. But I love my enemies and pray for those who
persecute me, that I may be a true son of my Father in
Heaven who causes His sun to rise on the evil and the
good and sends rain on the righteous and the
unrighteous. If I love only those who love me, what
reward will I get? Are not even the people of the world
doing that? If I greet only nice and courteous people,
what am I doing more than unbelievers? Do not even
worldly people do that? I become mature therefore, as
my heavenly Father is perfect. (Matt. 5:44-48 KJV
paraphrased).
                                      MEDITATIONS ON SELFLESSNESS

PAGE 2

 I am His disciple, I deny myself [I disregard, lose sight of,
and forget myself and my own interests]. I take up my
cross and follow Him [I cleave steadfastly to Him, conform
wholly to His example in living and, if need be, in dying,
also]. (Matt. 16:24 AMP).

I humble myself as a little child, so that I can be great in
the kingdom of heaven. (Matt. 18:4 KJV).

I do not exalt myself less I become abased; I humble
myself, so I can be exalted. (Matt. 23:12 KJV).

I do not seek the honour of men. I seek the honour of
God,so I do only as My Father bids me. If men do not
receive, recognize or approve me, I am not overly
concerned about human validation. How can I believe God
or please Him, if I seek the honour (recognition, praise,
approval, validation) of men and not the honor that comes
only from God? (John 5:41-45 KJV paraphrased).

I do not speak for myself, I do not seek glory for myself, I
seek to honour and glorify God, who sent me. So I speak
truth, not lies. I do things for the glory of God and not for
my own praise. (John 7:18 NKJV paraphrased).
                                      MEDITATIONS ON SELFLESSNESS

PAGE 3

Truly, except a corn of wheat falls into the ground and die,
it abides alone, but if it dies, it brings forth much fruit. As a
corn of wheat, I lay myself down to die so that through my
death, much fruit can be brought forth for God. I do not
seek to preserve my life (reputation, success, ministry,
wellbeing, ego, advancement), or else I shall lose them. I
disregard my life (my own interests, ambition, quests and
success) in this world, so I can keep them unto life eternal.
(John 12:24-25 KJV paraphrased, some additions mine).

I serve Jesus, I follow Him so that where He is, I also as His
servant can be, I do not seek the honour (recognition,
approval, praise) of men. I serve so that God the Father can
honour (recognise, lift and reward) me. (John 12: 26 KJV
paraphrased, some additions mine).

I do not think of myself more highly than I ought to think;
but I think soberly, according as God hath dealt to me a
measure of faith. (Rom. 12:3 KJV).

I love sincerely and selflessly. I hate what is evil; I cling to
what is good.I am devoted to others in brotherly love. I
honour others above myself and I prefer others above
myself. (Rom. 12:9-10 NIV).
                                       MEDITATIONS ON SELFLESSNESS

PAGE 4

I do not live for ‘myself’ and I do not die for ‘myself’. For as I
live, I live unto the Lord; and when I die, I die unto the Lord:
whether I live therefore, or die, I am the Lord's. (Rom. 14:7
– 8 KJV).

I do everything with the consciousness that I shall give
account of myself to God. (Rom. 14:12 KJV paraphrased).

I am spiritually strong, so I tolerate the infirmities
(wrongdoing, selfishness) of the weak. I do not live to
please myself. But I selflessly please others (my
neighbours) for their good to edification. For even Christ
(the most selfless), pleased not Himself; but, as it is
written, the reproaches of them that reproached thee fell
on me. (Rom. 15:1-3 AMP, some additions mine).

I am aware that it matters very little how I am evaluated by
men or by any human authority. I can't even trust my
judgment of myself on the issue of my faithfulness, well-
doing, motives and rightness/wrongness. It is the Lord
Himself who examines me, judges and decides. Therefore, I
live with the consciousness that my heart has to be right
and bare before the Lord who judges my hearts, motives,
thoughts, intentions, acts and ways. (1 Cor. 4: 3-4 NLT,
some additions mine).
                                       MEDITATIONS ON SELFLESSNESS

PAGE 5

I don't make judgments about anyone; It is only when the
Lord returns that He will bring all our darkest secrets to
light and reveal all our private motives. Then God will give
to each one whatever praise or rebuke that is due.
(1 Cor. 4:5 NLT).

I do not begin to commend myself. I do not, as some
others, arrange letters of commendation from men. I let
my life, humility, results and fruits in the lives of men be
my letter of commendation, written in their hearts, known
and read of all men. (2 Cor. 3:1-2 NKJV, paraphrased).

I am crucified with Christ; nevertheless, I live; yet not I, but
Christ lives in me: and the life which I now live in the flesh I
live by the faith of the Son of God, who loved me, and gave
himself for me. I do not frustrate the grace of God,for if
righteousness came by the law, then Christ is dead in vain.
(Gal. 2:20-21 KJV).

When other believers fall into sin, I gently and humbly help
them up to the right path. I am not judgmental, and I am
careful not to fall into the same temptation myself. I share
other people's burdens thereby obeying the law of Christ. I
don’t ever deem myself too important or big to serve
people. I see myself as nothing , God is the only one that
counts. (Gal. 6:1-3 NLT, some additions mine).
                                       MEDITATIONS ON SELFLESSNESS

PAGE 6

I imitate God as His dear child. I walk in love and
selflessness, as Christ also has selflessly loved me and has
given Himself for me, as an offering and a sacrifice to God
for a sweet-smelling aroma. (Eph. 5:1-2 NKJV).

I agree wholeheartedly with others, I love others. I work
together with others in one mind and purpose. I am not
selfish, I don't try to impress people. I am humble, I think
of others as better than myself. I don't look out only for my
interests, but I take an interest in others too.
(Phil. 2:2-4 NLT).

I make myself of no reputation, I take the form of a
servant, and I present myself in the likeness of a common
man. I humble myself and become obedient to God to the
point of death, I lay down my life for God and sacrifice
myself for others if need be. (Phil. 2:7-8 KJV).

I speak as a person approved by God and entrusted with
the gospel. I do not try to please men, but God, who tests
my heart. I never use flattery, nor do I put on a mask to
cover up greed, envy, bitterness and offences (God is my
witness). I do not look for praise from men, nor from you
or anyone else. I seek only the praise, commendation and
recognition that comes from God (1 Thess. 2:4-6 NLT).
